# Shared annotations and identities

Long explanations sometimes need information that should be available without
breaking the main argument. A reusable term may need a precise definition. A note
may add context that belongs beside the discussion rather than inside its sentence.
Screen and print present these additions differently, but they must agree about
which definition or note a reference names.

Literate therefore treats annotations as meaning before presentation. It collects
terms, notes, and uses into one renderer-neutral model. Screen renderers turn that
model into links, sections, asides, and indexes. Print turns the same identities into
page references, margin material, and indexes.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Source[Prose and annotation fences] --> Collect[Collect definitions, notes, and uses]
    Collect --> Resolve[Resolve keys to stable targets]
    Resolve --> Model[Shared annotation model]
    Model --> Screen[Screen links and indexes]
    Model --> Print[Page references and annotation rail]
```

The arrows mean that both media consume the same resolution result. They do not
mean that one presentation is translated into the other. The diagram omits styling
because appearance must not determine identity.

## Two kinds of annotation

A term has an author-chosen key, a display title, and a Markdown body. Its key lets
prose refer to the definition through a `term:` link. A note has a Markdown body but
no reusable key; its numbered occurrence is the reference point.

Both are fenced prose blocks rather than code fragments. They never contribute to a
generated source root. Their bodies remain in the book's semantic stream and are
parsed by a deliberately small annotation language.

A term declaration uses the shape `TERM key "Display title"`. Keys are
case-sensitive, begin with an ASCII letter, and continue with a restricted set of
letters, digits, and punctuation. The title is a JSON string, which gives escaping
one existing grammar instead of inventing another. A note uses the header `NOTE`
without arguments. Exact uppercase words reserve these forms; similar language
labels remain ordinary fences.

## Parse bodies once, then restrict them

Annotation bodies support paragraphs, ordered and unordered lists, emphasis,
strong emphasis, inline code, ordinary links, text, and line breaks. Headings,
block quotations, tables, images, raw HTML, code blocks, nested annotations, and
diagrams are rejected.

The parser is allowed to recognize disallowed structures so it can diagnose them.
Recognition is not authorization. This distinction is important for raw HTML: the
collector must see it in order to reject it, while renderers must never pass a
rejected body through unsanitized.

Uses inside accepted body prose are collected just like uses in ordinary prose.
Inline code, fenced code, and image alternatives are not term uses. The model keeps
token and child indexes for occurrences because the Markdown parser does not
provide exact source columns for every inline child. Locations remain honest rather
than pretending to precision the parser does not possess.

## Identity is not a visible label

Visible text is a poor identifier. Titles can be edited, repeated, translated, or
styled. Line numbers also move whenever prose is inserted. Shared destinations are
therefore generated in distinct namespaces from controlled semantic components.

The same fixed-width component encoding used by publication targets applies here:

```text
encoded component = concatenation of four hexadecimal digits per UTF-16 code unit
```

A unique term target derives from its key. This makes ordinary prose edits and a
move between chapters irrelevant to the term's identity. A note target derives
from its canonical source path and fence location, because a note has no author key.
Its displayed number derives from encounter order and is not its identity.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    TermKey[Term key] --> TermTarget[Stable term target]
    NotePlace[Document path and fence location] --> NoteTarget[Note target]
    BookOrder[Encounter order] --> NoteNumber[Displayed note number]
```

The arrows mean “determines.” The separation between note target and note number is
the point: renumbering a note for presentation does not redefine how its source
occurrence was identified. The diagram omits namespace prefixes, which are
mechanical collision protection.

`annotation identities and hrefs` separates encoded identity from visible wording.

```ts : <<annotation identities and hrefs>>=
const keyPattern = /^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{0,99}$/;
function encode(value: string): string {
    return value.split('').map(char => char.charCodeAt(0).toString(16).padStart(4, '0')).join('');
}
function termKey(href: string): string | undefined {
    if (!href.startsWith('term:')) { return undefined; }
    try { return decodeURIComponent(href.slice(5)); } catch { return undefined; }
}
export function resolveTermHref(annotations: PublicationAnnotations, href: string): string | undefined {
    const key = termKey(href);
    if (key === undefined || !keyPattern.test(key)) { return undefined; }
    const terms = annotations.terms.filter(term => term.key === key);
    return terms.length === 1 ? terms[0].target : undefined;
}
export function termByTarget(annotations: PublicationAnnotations, target: string): PublicationTerm | undefined {
    return annotations.terms.find(term => term.target === target);
}
```

## Collect before resolving

Forward references are normal in a book, so a use cannot be resolved when first
encountered. Collection proceeds in two passes:

1. gather every valid term, note, and `term:` use in document order;
2. group terms by key, diagnose duplicates, and resolve every use.

A key with one definition resolves. A key with no definition is missing. A key with
more than one definition is ambiguous. Ambiguity does not select the first term and
thereby make document order an accidental tie-breaker.

Duplicate term definitions still need distinct targets so a preview can show and
navigate to each diagnosed declaration. Location-qualified targets provide that
uniqueness. Only a term whose key is unique receives the shorter key-derived target.
Uses inside the term's own body are updated together when that identity is finalized.

Percent encoding in a `term:` destination is decoded exactly once. Malformed
encoding and keys outside the accepted grammar do not become lookup strings. A
failed lookup returns no target; it never echoes the author-provided destination as
if validation had succeeded.

## Occurrences have identities too

A definition target answers “where is this term defined?” An occurrence target
answers “which use is this index entry referring to?” These questions require
separate identities.

Each use records its owner and its token position within that owner. For ordinary
prose the owner is the document path. For annotation-body prose the owner is the
annotation target. Screen rendering derives a use anchor from this information, so
multiple equal labels remain independently addressable.

This is also why indexes do not search rendered text. Text search would confuse two
uses with the same label, miss formatting boundaries, and disagree between screen
and print. Explicit occurrence data preserves encounter order and exact semantic
membership.

## One meaning, different presentations

On screen, a term definition becomes an identified section and a note becomes an
identified aside. A resolved use becomes a link. A missing, ambiguous, or not-yet
available destination remains readable text with unresolved styling rather than a
misdirected link.

In print, a term definition remains in the prose. A resolved use links to it and can
show a page reference. Notes become numbered markers whose bodies are placed by the
annotation rail. Annotation bodies pass through the same controlled document
conversion as ordinary prose rather than being flattened into unformatted strings.

Only annotated books receive term and fragment indexes. The screen index links to
semantic targets and individual occurrences. The print index links to definition
and contribution pages. Neither index guesses targets from labels or scans final
markup.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Identity[Shared semantic target] --> Anchor[Screen anchor]
    Identity --> Capability[Editor navigation capability]
    Identity --> Label[Print destination and page reference]
    Anchor --> Index[Screen index]
    Label --> PrintIndex[Print index]
```

The arrows show adaptations of one identity. An editor capability is transport, not
a replacement identity. The diagram omits URLs and TeX syntax because those are
escaping concerns at later boundaries.

## Strict export, useful preview

Annotation diagnostics join project diagnostics. A durable export must not publish
an unresolved term, duplicate key, invalid header, or unsupported body. The editor
can still retain diagnosed annotation objects so the author sees the nearby prose
and can correct it.

This difference follows the publication principle established earlier: a preview
can be tolerant about incomplete work, while an export is a claim that references
are complete.

## Stability has limits

Generated identities are deterministic, but they are not universal permanent IDs.
Changing a term key intentionally changes its identity. Moving a note changes its
source-derived target. Editing earlier structure can change token positions used by
occurrence anchors. Stable here means independent of presentation and unrelated
prose changes within the stated components—not immune to edits that redefine those
components.

With shared identities established, the screen pipeline can focus on presentation:
how to merge syntax and semantic links, navigate between chapters, expose indexes,
and add optional exploration without making JavaScript a requirement for reading.
